"A395495 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A395495)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; A395495 ... Conjecture: a(n) = (n^2 - n + 6)/2 for n >= 4, with a(n) = 2n - 1 for n in {1, 2, 3}. ... Peter Exley, Solver code, data, and figures, GitHub.\n\n(Greetings from The On-Line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences!)\n\nA395495\n\nMinimum number of cells in a connected polyomino on the square grid that contains every fixed n-omino as a translated subregion.\n\n1\n\n1, 3, 5, 9, 13, 18, 24, 31, 39\n\n(list; graph; refs; listen; history; text; internal format)\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,2\n\nCOMMENTS\n\nContainment is translation-only: the contained piece may not be rotated or reflected. Number of fixed n-ominoes is A001168. Each of a(1) through a(9) is proved via SAT + CEGAR connectivity and carries a DRAT UNSAT certificate at k = a(n) - 1 that is independently verified by drat-trim. A pure-Python geometric containment verifier also re-confirms that every reported container does contain every fixed n-omino as a translate.\n\nConjecture: a(n) = (n^2 - n + 6)/2 for n >= 4, with a(n) = 2n - 1 for n in {1, 2, 3}. Equivalently a(n) = A000217(n-1) + 3 for n >= 4. Predicts a(10) = 48.\n\nAt n = 7 every minimum container has a single 1-cell interior hole. SAT model-enumeration over the 7 X 7 bounding box (the natural search box, which must contain both the horizontal and vertical n-cell I-omino) finds exactly four 24-cell containers; all four are the 25-cell discrete L_1 ball of radius 3 (centered in the 7 X 7 box) minus one cell from the D_4 orbit at L_1 distance 2 from the center, and all contain a hole. A wider 8 X 8 search at k = 23 returns UNSAT (LRAT certified). The hole is therefore a structural feature of a(7), distinguishing this sequence geometrically from the purely numerical match A350660. For n in {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9} the optimal container is simply connected.\n\nLINKS\n\nTable of n, a(n) for n=1..9.\n\nPeter Exley, Solver code, data, and figures, GitHub.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nOptimal containers, with one example per illustrated n. Cells are marked '#'; empty cells are '.'.\n\nThe 1 container with bounding box 3 X 3:\n\n. # .\n\n# # #\n\n. # .\n\n.\n\nThe 1 container with bounding box 4 X 4:\n\n. . # .\n\n. # # .\n\n# # # #\n\n. # # .\n\n.\n\nThe 1 container with bounding box 5 X 5:\n\n. . # . .\n\n. # # # .\n\n# # # # #\n\n. # # # .\n\n. . # . .\n\n.\n\nOne of 4 D_4-equivalent containers with bounding box 7 X 7 (the single dot at row 3 column 3 is the interior hole):\n\n. . . # . . .\n\n. . # # # . .\n\n. # . # # # .\n\n# # # # # # #\n\n. # # # # # .\n\n. . # # # . .\n\n. . . # . . .\n\n.\n\nThe 1 container with bounding box 9 X 9:\n\n. . . . . # . . .\n\n. . . . # # . . .\n\n. . . # # # . . .\n\n. . # # # # # # #\n\n. # # # # # # # #\n\n# # # # # # # # #\n\n. . . # # # . . .\n\n. . . # # # . . .\n\n. . . # # # . . .\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A327094 (the free-piece analog), A001168 (number of fixed n-ominoes (the family contained)), A000105 (number of free n-ominoes), A000217.\n\nSequence in context: A024403 A129230 A203567 * A350660 A071404 A074133\n\nAdjacent sequences: A395492 A395493 A395494 * A395496 A395497 A395498\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,more\n\nAUTHOR\n\nPeter Exley, Apr 26 2026--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pexley-math/oeis-A352029: Paper and figures for OEIS A352029 -- a(n) is the number of minimalist polyominoes that can contain all n-ominoes. · GitHub (https://github.com/pexley-math/oeis-A352029)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; > `a(n)` is the number of distinct free polyominoes of the minimum size A327094(`n`) that contain every free n-omino. ... The size `k = ` A327094(`n`) is supplied by the companion sequence; given it, a universal container is exactly a `k`-cell connected shape into which all free n-ominoes embed.\nA polyomino `C` is a container for the free n-ominoes if every free n-omino fits inside `C` under some rotation, reflection, and shift. The fewest cells such a container can have is the minimum container size -- this is OEIS A327094 (Kagey, 2019). Usually several distinct shapes achieve that minimum, and counting them is our question:\n\n> `a(n)` is the number of distinct free polyominoes of the minimum size A327094(`n`) that contain every free n-omino.\n\nThis is OEIS A352029 (Mason, 2022). Mason recorded the first seven values; this work adds the eighth, `a(8) = 7`, and gives a single self-contained account of all eight.\n\n## Definitions\n\nWe use the following terms throughout.\n\n  * Free n-omino. A connected set of `n` cells on the square lattice, taken up to the eight symmetries of the square (four rotations and four reflections) together with translation. The number of free n-ominoes is OEIS A000105.\n  * Container. A connected polyomino `C` such that every free n-omino can be placed inside `C` by some rotation, reflection, and translation.\n  * Minimum container size `k`. The least cell count of any container for the free n-ominoes, `k = ` A327094(`n`).\n  * `a(n)`. The number of containers of exactly `k` cells, counted up to the square symmetries and translation.\n  * Bounding box `(H, W)`. The tightest `H`-by-`W` rectangle enclosing a shape. We organise the search by bounding box, covering every shape once.\n\n## The values\n\nResult. For `n = 1` to `8`, the number of minimum-size universal containers is:\n\n`n`  | 1  | 2  | 3  | 4  | 5  | 6  | 7  | 8\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n`a(n)`  | 1  | 1  | 2  | 2  | 2  | 14  | 204  | 7\n\nwith minimum container sizes `k =` A327094(`n`):\n\n`n`  | 1  | 2  | 3  | 4  | 5  | 6  | 7  | 8\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n`k`  | 1  | 2  | 4  | 6  | 9  | 12  | 17  | 20\n\nEach value is established two independent ways (next section); the two agree for every `n` from 1 to 8. The container shapes themselves are drawn in the gallery, `figures.pdf`: they grow from the single cell at `n = 1` to the seven compact twenty-cell octomino containers at `n = 8`, which sit in `4`-by-`8` or `5`-by-`8` boxes.\n\nWe confirm each value with an independent verifier that shares no code with the solver. It re-checks, from scratch, that every reported container has the right size, is connected, holds all free n-ominoes, and is genuinely minimal (no cell can be removed), and that the containers are pairwise distinct. For the small cases (`n <= 5`) it also enumerates all `k`-cell shapes directly and counts the universal ones, recovering `a(n)` with no reference to the solver at all. The two methods agree on all eight terms.\n\n## Patterns and open questions\n\nThe sequence is sharply non-monotone: it climbs to `a(7) = 204` and then collapses to `a(8) = 7`. The reason is constraint density. The number of free n-ominoes that must all fit, A000105(`n`), grows exponentially, while the container size A327094(`n`) grows only sub-quadratically, so the pieces-per-cell ratio rises steadily -- about `6.4` at `n = 7` but `18.5` at `n = 8`. Once the density is that high, the demand that all `369` free octominoes co-embed in a single twenty-cell shape is so tight that only seven shapes survive.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA395494 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A395494)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Adjacent sequences: A395491 A395492 A395493 * A395495 A395496 A395497\n\nA395494\n\nPrimitive zeros of A395183.\n\n1\n\n0, 111, 123, 159, 189, 239, 259, 271, 279, 297, 311, 357, 479, 481, 497, 551, 561, 671, 711, 719, 757, 831, 833, 839, 871, 951, 957, 979, 1023, 1057, 1111, 1147, 1151, 1161, 1209, 1231, 1241, 1271, 1309, 1313, 1319, 1321, 1333, 1337, 1387, 1391, 1417, 1419, 1421\n\nOFFSET\n\n1,2\n\nCOMMENTS\n\nNumbers m such that A395183(m) = 0 and A395183(d) != 0 for all proper divisors d of m.\n\nA395183(k) = 0 if and only if k is a multiple of some