g holes (Parkin et al. 1967, Madachy 1969, Golomb 1994) for the first few Image: n.\n\nImage: n  | name  | free  | one-sided  | fixed  | with holes\nSloane  |  | A000105  | A000988  | A001168  | A001419\n1  | monomino  | 1  | 1  | 1  | 0\n2  | domino  | 1  | 1  | 2  | 0\n3  | triomino  | 2  | 2  | 6  | 0\n4  | tetromino  | 5  | 7  | 19  | 0\n5  | pentomino  | 12  | 18  | 63  | 0\n6  | hexomino  | 35  | 60  | 216  | 0\n7  | heptomino  | 108  | 196  | 760  | 1\n8  | octomino  | 369  | 704  | 2725  | 6\n9  |  | 1285  | 2500  | 9910  | 37\n10  |  | 4655  | 9189  | 36446  | 195\n11  |  | 17073  | 33896  | 135268  | 979\n12  |  | 63600  | 126759  | 505861  | 4663\n13  |  | 238591  | 476270  | 1903890  | 21474\n14  |  | 901971  | 1802312  | 7204874  | 96496\n15  |  | 3426576  | 6849777  | 27394666  | 425449\n\nIf Image: P(n) is the number of free Image: n-polyominoes, then the limit\n\nImage: lambda=lim_(n->infty)P(n)^(1/n) \n\nexists and is the same for free, one-sided, and fixed polyominoes (Klarner 1967). Bounds on Image: lambda, rather than bounds on Image: P(n) valid for every Image: n, include\n\nImage: 4.00253<lambda<=4.5238. \n\nThe lower bound is due to Barequet et al. (2016), and the upper bound to Bui (2026).\n\nGeneralizations of polyominoes to base shapes other than squares are known as polyforms, with the best-known examples being the polyiamonds and polyhexes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSAFETY DATA SHEET (https://nipponpaint.com.sg/wp-content/uploads/OTH-0289_FLOOR-PRO-914-SF-EPOXY-PUTTY-PART-B_SDS.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; P501 Dispose of content/container to appropriate waste site or reclaimer in accordance with local or ... Polyomino Amide \n112-57-2\nFLOOR-PRO 914 SF EPOXY PUTTY PART B \n \n \n \nResponse \nP302+P352: IF ON SKIN: Wash with plenty of soap and water  \nP332+P313: If skin irritation occurs: Get medical advice/attention P \n337+P313: If eye irrit